# Scaling Principle

The Data Pipeline Architecture (Weekly Breakdown)
[WEEK 1: Universe Gathering] ──> Target: 4,000 Raw Leads (Scraping DSIR, KIADB, Trade Exhibitions)
                                        │
                                        ▼
[WEEK 2: Automated Filtration] ──> Apply Eligibility Gates (Revenue API + Keyword Filter) ──> 1,500 Remaining
                                        │
                                        ▼
[WEEK 3: Deep LLM Qualification] ──> Gemini/Claude Parsing for Moats (C3) & Systems (C7) ──> 1,100 Remaining
                                        │
                                        ▼
[WEEK 4: Human-in-the-Loop QC] ──> Manual edge-case checks & first-line hook building ──> 1,000 Final Pass
Week 1: Sourcing the Initial Universe (Target: 4,000 Raw Entries)
Objective: Cast a wide, structural net across our selected industrial baskets.

Execution: Run Python scripts using libraries like BeautifulSoup and Selenium to extract raw exporter tables, historical B2B trade show catalogs, and state industrial development corporation allocation logs.

Output: A centralized, raw master directory containing roughly 4,000 company names, locations, and website domain links.

Week 2: Automated Qualification & Financial Gates (Target: 1,500 Surviving Companies)
Objective: Eliminate corporate conglomerates, small shops, traders, and pure services firms.

Execution: 1. Pipe the 4,000 company names into financial verification APIs (such as Tofler or Zauba API integrations) to check current revenue brackets. Instantly eliminate any firm exceeding the ₹500Cr ceiling or falling beneath ₹10Cr.
2. Execute a rapid script to ping corporate websites. If the URL returns a dead 404, an unrenewed domain screen, or is a single-page blank placeholder, delete the row automatically.

Expected Stage Yield: ~35–40% transition rate due to the strict revenue ceiling and trader elimination.

Week 3: Deep LLM Analysis & Feature Scoring (Target: 1,100 Surviving Companies)
Objective: Evaluate the subtle "Federer" indicators like technical differentiation and software usage.

Execution: Use an orchestrator framework (like Python's asyncio coupled with the Gemini/Claude API) to crawl the "About Us," "Products," and "Careers" pages of the remaining 1,500 websites. The LLM will be fed highly structured prompts instructing it to search for explicit capability markers:

Does the text contain system keywords like SAP, ERP, LIMS, or ISO/IEC certifications?

Is the product line custom and technical, or bulk/commodity?

Does the company list professional division heads (CFO, COO) or second-generation leaders?

Expected Stage Yield: ~70–75% accuracy rate, moving high-confidence matches down the funnel.

Week 4: Human-in-the-Loop Quality Control & Final Hook Construction (Target: 1,000 Final ICP List)
Objective: Remove false positives, double-check edge cases, and craft the high-conversion outreach lines.

Execution: An analytics fellow reviews borderline flags (companies scoring between 40-59 on the automated layout). A final verification python script flattens out internal syntax errors, and validates that every company contains a true, non-templated first-line email hook focusing on real physical assets (such as plant capacity additions or patent numbers).

Final Output: A pristine, production-ready, fully verified master dataset containing exactly 1,000 validated Federer targets.
